  <p class="lead"><strong>Looking for DQ cake prices and sizes?</strong> They are on our <a href="/categories/dq-cakes">Dairy Queen cake prices and sizes</a> page, with a table for every size. This guide covers what happens after you choose a size: picking a flavor, what is inside the cake, and how to cut, serve and store it so it does not turn into a puddle.</p>

  <h2 id="whats-inside">What is inside a Dairy Queen cake?</h2>
  <p>A classic DQ cake has three parts:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Chocolate soft serve</strong> on the bottom.</li>
    <li><strong>The crunch layer</strong> in the middle: chocolate cookie pieces mixed with fudge. This is the part everyone fights over.</li>
    <li><strong>Vanilla soft serve</strong> on top, covered with whipped icing and decorations.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>There is no sponge cake, so it is all frozen. That is why it needs a few minutes on the counter before you cut it.</p>

  <h2 id="flavors">Classic cake or Blizzard cake?</h2>
  <p>A <strong>classic DQ cake</strong> is chocolate and vanilla with the crunch center. It is the safest pick for kids and mixed groups, and it costs a few dollars less.</p>
  <p>A <strong>Blizzard cake</strong> blends a Blizzard flavor into the soft serve. The <a href="/items/oreo-blizzard-cake">Oreo Blizzard Cake</a> is the most popular, followed by <a href="/items/cookie-dough-blizzard-cake">Cookie Dough</a>. Choose a Blizzard cake if the group already has a favorite Blizzard. Not sure which one? See our <a href="/blog/best-blizzard-flavors-ranked">Blizzard flavor rankings</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="how-many-slices">How many slices will you get?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/items/dq-cake-round-8inch">8-inch round</a>: about 8 to 10 slices.</li>
    <li><a href="/items/dq-cake-round-10inch">10-inch round</a>: about 12 to 16 slices.</li>
    <li><a href="/items/dq-sheet-cake">Sheet cake</a>: about 20 to 25 slices.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Kids’ parties go further with thinner slices. If adults are the main crowd, plan on the lower number.</p>

  <h2 id="cutting">How to cut a DQ cake cleanly</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Let it sit 10 to 15 minutes</strong> on the counter. Cut it straight from the freezer and the crunch layer can shatter.</li>
    <li><strong>Warm the knife</strong> in a tall glass of hot water for about 20 seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Wipe it dry</strong> before each cut so water does not freeze on the icing.</li>
    <li><strong>Press straight down</strong> in one motion instead of sawing. Rewarm the knife between slices.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="storing">Storing leftovers</h2>
  <p>Put the cake back in the freezer as soon as everyone is served. Cover the cut side with plastic wrap or keep it in the box. It stays good for about a week; after that the icing can get icy.</p>

  <h2 id="ordering">Ordering tips</h2>
  <p>Ready-made cakes are usually in the freezer case for same-day pickup, and staff can add a short message. Custom designs, photo cakes and Blizzard flavors that are not in the case need about 24 to 48 hours. The full ordering steps are on our <a href="/categories/dq-cakes#how-to-order">DQ cake ordering guide</a>.</p>
